===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|deflexion}} {{qualifier|British}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|dēflexiō}}, from {{m|la|dēflectō|dēflectere}} (“to deflect”; participle stem ''dēflex-'') + {{m|la|-iō|pos=suffix forming abstract nouns}}. The non-etymological spelling ''deflection'' is taken from the present stem {{m|la||dēflect-}}, associated with {{m|la|collection}}, {{m|la|dissection}}, etc.<ref>{{R:OED2|id=00059570}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/dɪˈflɛkʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-deflection.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/dəˈflɛkʃən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkʃən|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of [[deflecting]] or something [[deflected]].
#: {{ux|en|Russell's goalbound shot took a '''deflection''' off a defender and went out for a corner.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1918|author=H. G. Wells|title=Joan and Peter
|passage=The next morning Oswald stopped short in the middle of his shaving, which in his case involved the most tortuous '''deflections''' and grimacings.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=March|author=H. A. Vallance|title=On Foot Across the Forth Bridge|journal=Railway Magazine|page=148|text=The structure was examined and passed by the Board of Trade in February, 1890, after it had been tested with two trains of 50 loaded wagons, each hauled by two engines, and pushed by a third. The trains ran side by side, and their combined weight, including the six engines, was 1,800 tons. The '''deflections''' of the girders were precisely in accordance with the calculations of the engineers.}}   
# The [[deviation]] of a [[needle]] or other [[indicator]] from its previous [[position]].

